  (:adr/authoritative false)
  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799008156: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8156 Shoemaking and Related Machine Operators plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8156\n-- Shoemaking and Related Machine Operators** only. This is Wave3\n(production/trades); Wave0/1/2 are 100% complete, and this actor\ncontinues the 81xx plant-and-machine-operator range\n(`kotoba.occupation.wave/wave-of \"8156\"` resolves to wave 3, verified\nlive against a fresh clone before any work began).\n\n`gh api repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8156` returned a clean\n404 before any work began -- this was a genuinely fresh scaffold, not a\nresume of prior partial work.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8156\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"8156\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Shoemaking and Related Machine\nOperators\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`.\nThis ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `8156` entry to\n`:implemented`.\n\n**Industrial footwear-machine hazard dimension.** Shoemaking and\nRelated Machine Operators run cutting/stitching/molding machinery for\nfootwear manufacture (cutting-die presses, stitching machines,\nsole-molding/lasting machines) -- a real heavy-machinery hazard domain:\ncrush and cutting-blade injury from presses and cutting dies, plus\nadhesive-fume exposure. This actor does NOT model direct\nshoemaking-machine operation authority or plant-safety-clearance\nauthority. It is deliberately restricted to PLANT SCHEDULING and\nLOGISTICS COORDINATION around the occupation (production-run/\ninventory/progress-record logging, crew/shift scheduling, safety-\nconcern escalation, and leather/sole/adhesive-materials procurement\ncoordination) -- never the machine-operation-execution decision or the\nplant-safety-clearance decision itself, and never an override of a\nplant safety officer's judgment. The closed proposal-op allowlist\nstructurally excludes any op that could directly finalize such a\ndecision, and the governor independently, permanently hard-blocks any\nproposal whose rationale text names such a finalization action,\nregardless of the declared `:op` or confidence -- this is not an\nescalation path a human can approve through, it is an outright,\nstructural refusal at every maturity level.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored closely for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8143`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8143)\n(Paper Products Machine Operators -- a closely related cutting/\nconverting industrial-machine safety pattern), independently re-cloned\nand read in full this session, and independently re-verified as green\n(fresh clone, `clojure -M:test`) before being used as a template. Its\n`papercoord.{store,advisor,governor,actor}` module split, `langgraph.\ngraph` StateGraph shape, closed op-allowlist and scope-exclusion\nself-trip-guard design translate almost 1:1 onto this occupation's own\nheavy-machinery-hazard-adjacent coordination scope, so this ADR's\n`shoecoord.*` modules follow the same shape with domain terms\nsubstituted (converter -> operator, paper-converting facility/plant ->\nfootwear manufacturing facility/plant, converting/cutting/folding run\n-> pressing/cutting/stitching/molding run, machine-operation-execution\ndecision stays the same class of decision).\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8156` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for shoemaking and related\nmachine operators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as\nevery prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Shoemaking\nand Related Machine Operators Plant Scheduling Coordination Advisor ⊣\nShoeCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.\nkotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the reference's\n`deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj`\nused by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent\nGovernor, human-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an\nappend-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **plant scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never shoemaking-machine operation authority** -- it must NEVER\nbe designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a\nmachine-operation-execution decision or a plant-safety-clearance\ndecision.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/inventory/progress data\n  logging only\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n  only\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a machinery-hazard/adhesive-fume-\n  exposure/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately,\n  NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- leather/sole/adhesive-materials\n  procurement proposal only\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase,\nresembles a machine-operation-execution finalization decision or a\nplant-safety-clearance finalization decision -- these are structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely, not merely gated behind\nescalation.\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Operator unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered operator (crew member) record\n   (`:no-operator`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the\n   operator is.\n2. **Facility unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered footwear manufacturing facility/line\n   record (`:no-facility`).\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (`:no-actuation`; the governor never dispatches hardware\n   itself and never operates shoemaking machinery itself).\n4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`).\n5. **Scope-excluded action** -- the proposal's `:op` or `:rationale`\n   must never name a machine-operation-execution finalization, a\n   plant-safety-clearance finalization, or a plant-safety-officer-\n   judgment override (`:scope-excluded-action`), REGARDLESS of the\n   declared `:op` or confidence. This is a hard, PERMANENT block --\n   there is no maturity level or confidence at which this actor may\n   auto-commit or human-approve its way past this rule; it is an\n   outright refusal, not an escalation.\n\n### Escalation gates (always human sign-off)\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's always-escalate path). The only channel by\n  which the robot may surface a press-crush/cutting-die-injury/\n  adhesive-fume-exposure/equipment-condition observation -- it never\n  resolves the concern itself.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000) -- always needs human sign-off,\n  independent of confidence (boundary verified as exclusive by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`: exactly-at-threshold\n  commits, over-threshold escalates).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, CLAUDE.md)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare NOUNS (e.g.\n\"shoe\", \"adhesive\", \"cutting die\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- and this vertical's domain\nvocabulary is an especially sharp case of that risk, because routine\nwork-log/task text legitimately names shoemaking machinery by name\n(e.g. a task literally described as \"routine shoemaking task with\nleather uppers and cutting dies\" -- equipment identification, not a\nproposal to finalize a shoemaking-machine operation or clear the plant\nas safe). `shoecoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization ACTION PHRASES (\"proceed with the pressing\nrun\", \"finalize the shoemaking-machine operation\", \"declare the plant\nsafety cleared\", \"override the plant safety officer's judgment\"),\nnever bare nouns, and is checked only against the proposal's\n`:rationale` field (the advisor's own generated text), never against\nraw `:task` input text. `shoecoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-\nproposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated\nregression test driving the default mock-advisor across all four\nallowed ops with a task field containing exactly those bare equipment\nnouns, asserting none of them ever hard-trip the finalization-scope\nrule. Green on first run.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8156\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8156` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8156`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before the edit,\n  not assumed, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  6-agent batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 30 tests, 65 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/\n  counts` and independently re-verified against a brand-new fresh\n  clone of the pushed repository (not just the local working copy). No\n  mojibake in the Japanese violation-detail strings (0\n  replacement-byte instances grepped in the fresh clone).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-shoemaking-machine-run`/\n  `:approve-plant-safety-clearance` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate path: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to\n  finalize a shoemaking-machine operation or a plant-safety clearance\n  exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For\n  a decision whose failure mode is crush/cutting-blade injury, the\n  capability itself must not exist in this actor at all.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"shoe\" \"adhesive\"\n  \"cutting die\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip\n  bug class described above -- this domain's own vocabulary\n  necessarily discusses shoemaking machinery by name to do its\n  logging/scheduling job, so a bare-noun list would self-block the\n  actor's own legitimate proposals whenever a task description\n  mentions leather, adhesive or cutting dies.\n- Naming the crew-member field `:converter-id` (mirroring the\n  reference repo's own field name literally): rejected -- \"converter\"\n  is the paper-industry term for the reference occupation's crew\n  member and has no meaning in footwear manufacturing; `:operator-id`\n  (matching the ISCO title \"...Machine Operators\" literally) is used\n  instead, since this fleet's \"operator\" collision risk (deploying\n  business/entity vs. individual crew member) that motivated the\n  reference repo's naming choice does not recur here in a way that\n  creates ambiguity within this repo's own docs.\n\n## Test verification\n\nFresh local run immediately before pushing, and independently\nreproduced against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository:\n\n```text\nRunning tests in #{\"test\"}\n\nTesting shoecoord.actor-test\n\nTesting shoecoord.governor-test\n\nRan 30 tests containing 65 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\n```\n")))
